@anon.6e01a69b2c234dfdc5e23dbc943ea0bc.1346 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [5449 x i8] }> <{ [5449 x i8] c"Manages stateful parts of your document.\0A\0ALet's say you have some computations in your document and want to remember\0Athe result of your last computation to use it in the next one. You might try\0Asomething similar to the code below and expect it to output 10, 13, 26, and\0A21. However this **does not work** in Typst. If you test this code, you will\0Asee that Typst complains with the following error message: _Variables from\0Aoutside the function are read-only and cannot be modified._\0A\0A```typ\0A// This doesn't work!\0A#let x = 0\0A#let compute(expr) = {\0A  x = eval(\0A    expr.replace(\22x\22, str(x))\0A  )\0A  [New value is #x. ]\0A}\0A\0A#compute(\2210\22) \\\0A#compute(\22x + 3\22) \\\0A#compute(\22x * 2\22) \\\0A#compute(\22x - 5\22)\0A```\0A\0A# State and document markup { #state-and-markup }\0AWhy does it do that? Because, in general, this kind of computation with side\0Aeffects is problematic in document markup and Typst is upfront about that.\0AFor the results to make sense, the computation must proceed in the same\0Aorder in which the results will be laid out in the document. In our simple\0Aexample, that's the case, but in general it might not be.\0A\0ALet's look at a slightly different, but similar kind of state: The heading\0Anumbering. We want to increase the heading counter at each heading. Easy\0Aenough, right? Just add one. Well, it's not that simple. Consider the\0Afollowing example:\0A\0A```example\0A#set heading(numbering: \221.\22)\0A#let template(body) = [\0A  = Outline\0A  ...\0A  #body\0A]\0A\0A#show: template\0A\0A= Introduction\0A...\0A```\0A\0AHere, Typst first processes the body of the document after the show rule,\0Asees the `Introduction` heading, then passes the resulting content to the\0A`template` function and only then sees the `Outline`. Just counting up would\0Anumber the `Introduction` with `1` and the `Outline` with `2`.\0A\0A# Managing state in Typst { #state-in-typst }\0ASo what do we do instead? We use Typst's state management system. Calling\0Athe `state` function with an identifying string key and an optional initial\0Avalue gives you a state value which exposes a few function. The two most\0Aimportant ones are `get` and `update`:\0A\0A- The [`get`]($state.get) function retrieves the current value of the state.\0A  Because the value can vary over the course of the document, it is a\0A  _contextual_ function that can only be used when [context]($context) is\0A  available.\0A\0A- The [`update`]($state.update) function modifies the state. You can give it\0A  any value. If given a non-function value, it sets the state to that value.\0A  If given a function, that function receives the previous state and has to\0A  return the new state.\0A\0AOur initial example would now look like this:\0A\0A```example\0A#let s = state(\22x\22, 0)\0A#let compute(expr) = [\0A  #s.update(x =>\0A    eval(expr.replace(\22x\22, str(x)))\0A  )\0A  New value is #context s.get().\0A]\0A\0A#compute(\2210\22) \\\0A#compute(\22x + 3\22) \\\0A#compute(\22x * 2\22) \\\0A#compute(\22x - 5\22)\0A```\0A\0AState managed by Typst is always updated in layout order, not in evaluation\0Aorder. The `update` method returns content and its effect occurs at the\0Aposition where the returned content is inserted into the document.\0A\0AAs a result, we can now also store some of the computations in variables,\0Abut they still show the correct results:\0A\0A```example\0A>>> #let s = state(\22x\22, 0)\0A>>> #let compute(expr) = [\0A>>>   #s.update(x =>\0A>>>     eval(expr.replace(\22x\22, str(x)))\0A>>>   )\0A>>>   New value is #context s.get().\0A>>> ]\0A<<< ...\0A\0A#let more = [\0A  #compute(\22x * 2\22) \\\0A  #compute(\22x - 5\22)\0A]\0A\0A#compute(\2210\22) \\\0A#compute(\22x + 3\22) \\\0A#more\0A```\0A\0AThis example is of course a bit silly, but in practice this is often exactly\0Awhat you want! A good example are heading counters, which is why Typst's\0A[counting system]($counter) is very similar to its state system.\0A\0A# Time Travel\0ABy using Typst's state management system you also get time travel\0Acapabilities! We can find out what the value of the state will be at any\0Aposition in the document from anywhere else. In particular, the `at` method\0Agives us the value of the state at any particular location and the `final`\0Amethods gives us the value of the state at the end of the document.\0A\0A```example\0A>>> #let s = state(\22x\22, 0)\0A>>> #let compute(expr) = [\0A>>>   #s.update(x => {\0A>>>     eval(expr.replace(\22x\22, str(x)))\0A>>>   })\0A>>>   New value is #context s.get().\0A>>> ]\0A<<< ...\0A\0AValue at `<here>` is\0A#context s.at(<here>)\0A\0A#compute(\2210\22) \\\0A#compute(\22x + 3\22) \\\0A*Here.* <here> \\\0A#compute(\22x * 2\22) \\\0A#compute(\22x - 5\22)\0A```\0A\0A# A word of caution { #caution }\0ATo resolve the values of all states, Typst evaluates parts of your code\0Amultiple times. However, there is no guarantee that your state manipulation\0Acan actually be completely resolved.\0A\0AFor instance, if you generate state updates depending on the final value of\0Aa state, the results might never converge. The example below illustrates\0Athis. We initialize our state with `1` and then update it to its own final\0Avalue plus 1. So it should be `2`, but then its final value is `2`, so it\0Ashould be `3`, and so on. This example displays a finite value because Typst\0Asimply gives up after a few attempts.\0A\0A```example\0A// This is bad!\0A#let s = state(\22x\22, 1)\0A#context s.update(s.final() + 1)\0A#context s.get()\0A```\0A\0AIn general, you should try not to generate state updates from within context\0Aexpressions. If possible, try to express your updates as non-contextual\0Avalues or functions that compute the new value from the previous value.\0ASometimes, it cannot be helped, but in those cases it is up to you to ensure\0Athat the result converges." }>, align 1
